From 810d99becb929f25d329c6f20e30f762f6ae2c67 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mohammad Rafi Shaik <mohammad.rafi.shaik@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:10:27 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 045/100] PENDING: ASoC: codecs: lpass-macro: add codec version
 2.9 and

fix register cache handling

Add LPASS macro codec support for codec version 2.9 and correct several
register cache issues affecting all four macro codecs.

Codec version handling in lpass-rx-macro:
- Handle LPASS_CODEC_VERSION_2_9 in the version based switch statements
so it is treated the same as the other members of the 2.5 family,
which is what Glymur requires. This covers the read-write register
check, the control and widget selection at component probe, and the
register stride and default table selection at probe.
- Add LPASS_MACRO_FLAG_HAS_NPL_CLOCK to the sm8550 match entry. The
missing flag left sm8550 running without its NPL clock, which caused
sporadic playback stalls.

Register default sorting in lpass-rx-macro and lpass-wsa-macro:
- Both drivers build the register defaults array by concatenating a
base table with a codec version specific one, so the result is not in
register address order because the version specific ranges overlap
the base range. Regmap detects this, warns that the driver needs
fixing, and sorts its own copy at runtime. Sort the concatenated
array before handing it to regmap so the warning goes away and regmap
does not sort a second time. The base and version specific split is
kept deliberately for readability, so a static reorder is not
appropriate here.

Cache type for all four macro codecs:
- Switch the register cache from REGCACHE_FLAT to REGCACHE_MAPLE in
lpass-rx-macro, lpass-wsa-macro, lpass-tx-macro and lpass-va-macro.
A flat cache hides missing default reads by returning the zero
initialised cache slot instead of performing a bus read, and the
caller cannot tell that value apart from a real hardware value, so
any readable non-volatile register without a default silently returns
wrong data. With a maple cache a miss returns -ENOENT and the regmap
core falls back to a real bus read, so the caller always observes the
true hardware value. Write cache behaviour is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Mohammad Rafi Shaik <mohammad.rafi.shaik@oss.qualcomm.com>
